<h1>About <em>Us</em></h1>
<p class="hero-sub">Free, data-backed tools that answer the questions athletes, parents, and coaches actually ask: how do my numbers compare, and what's realistic to work toward next?</p>
</div><div class="content-wrap">
<h2>What We Build</h2>
<p>Athlete Calculator is a set of free, no-signup tools for measuring and understanding athletic performance. Our calculators cover vertical jump percentile, sprint and 40-yard dash speed, squat strength standards, broad jump distance, and dunk eligibility — each one built to answer a specific, practical question rather than to just spit out a raw number.</p>
<p>Every calculator is paired with reference data pulled from combine results, sport-specific research, and age-based normative charts, so a result comes with context: not just "your vertical jump is 28 inches," but where that number sits relative to your age, sport, and level of competition.</p>
<h2>Why We Started This</h2>
<p>Most athletic testing calculators online are either outdated, missing context, or built around a single generic standard that doesn't account for age, sport, or position. We built Athlete Calculator to fix that: current data, clear percentile breakdowns, and tools that work whether you're a 14-year-old checking your vertical jump against your age group or a coach benchmarking a roster against combine standards.</p>
<p>Alongside the calculators, we publish regular articles and breakdowns covering combine results, training camp storylines, and the athletic numbers behind current sports news — because the tools are more useful with real context behind them.</p>
<div class="callout">
<strong>Who This Is For</strong>
    Youth and high school athletes tracking progress, parents trying to understand where their kid stands, coaches benchmarking a team, and anyone curious how their own numbers compare to combine-level standards.
  </div>
<h2>How the Numbers Work</h2>
<p>Our percentile and standard charts are built from publicly available combine data, peer-reviewed sport science research, and established age-based normative tables. We update these regularly as new combine classes and research come out, and we link to source context wherever it's useful. We're not a substitute for professional coaching or medical advice — think of these tools as a fast, free way to get oriented, not a replacement for individualized training guidance.</p>
<h2>Get in Touch</h2>
<p>Questions, corrections, or a data point you think we got wrong? Visit our <a class="inline-link" href="/contact">contact page</a> — we read everything and update calculators when we find a better source.</p>
